  abstract={Prices of crude oil are renowned for being volatile, and they are governed by a broad set of factors including global economic scenarios, political tensions, and supply-demand ratios. Precise forecasting of the prices of crude oil is pivotal for investors, policymakers, as well as for business players, so that appropriate choices can be made. Here, we propose a hybrid machine learning (ML) model to enhance precision in forecasting prices of crude oil. In the current work, individual models such as Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) and Support Vector Regression (SVR) along with hybrid models integrating SVR with K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Forest (RF), and Decision Tree (DT) are researched. Training as well as testing are conducted for the historical sets of Brent Crude, WTI Crude, and OPEC Basket data between the years 2019-2024. The findings establish that an individual SVR model outperforms ARIMA, with a score of 0.0511 MAE and 0.0683 RMSE to estimate the price of Brent Crude. In the instance of hybrid models, SVR+KNN signifies the minimum value of 0.3115 MAPE, outpacing SVR+RF as well as SVR+DT. Overall, SVR signals high prediction value for different time scales, thereby underscoring machine learning's capabilities to model intricate, non-linear financial time series.},
