  pages={1-10},
  abstract={Macroeconomic time-series forecasting in emerging market economies is complicated by non-stationarity, regime-switching volatility, structural breaks, fat-tailed distributions, and long-range temporal dependence. Classical econometric models like Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) enforce strict integer differencing $(d \in\{0,1\})$, risking overdifferencing and the destruction of low-frequency equilibrium signals. Conversely, standalone non-linear architectures like Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) often overfit high-frequency multi-scale noise. This paper presents a unified econometric framework: the Hybrid Fractional Wavelet Neural Network (ARFIMA-DWT-NARX) model. By integrating Autoregressive Fractionally Integrated Moving Average with Exogenous Covariates (ARFIMAX), Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) multi-resolution analysis, and specialized Nonlinear Autoregressive with Exogenous Inputs (NARX) sub-networks, the architecture decouples persistent linear dynamics from scale-specific non-linear volatility. Evaluated on 180 monthly observations (2011–2025) across six core Indian economic indicators, the proposed framework demonstrates superior out-of-sample predictive performance $\left(R^{2}\gt0.94\right)$, reducing Mean Squared Error (MSE) by up to 89Keywords: Inflation Rate, Exchange Rate, Crude Oil Prices, Real GDP Growth, ARFIMAX, Fractional Integration, Discrete Wavelet Transform, NARX Neural Network, Long Memory.},
